    "---\n",
    "title: \"Python Modules and Packages\"\n",
    "format: html\n",
    "toc: true\n",
    "editor: visual\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Introduction\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Modules\n",
    "\n",
    "A module is a file containing a set of code or a set of functions which can be included in an application. A module could be a file containing a single variable, a function, or a large codebase. This file has a `.py` extension.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "-   [Random](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/python-random-module/)\n",
    "-   [Math](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/python-math-module/)\n",
    "-   [Statistics](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/statistics-with-python/)\\\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Packages\n",
    "\n",
    "A package is a directory containing collections of modules. The directory contains Python modules and also having `__init__.py` file by which the interpreter interprets it as a Package.\n",
    "\n",
    "Examples:\n",
    "\n",
    "-   [Numpy](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/numpy/python-numpy/)\n",
    "-   [Pandas](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/pandas/pandas-tutorial/)\n",
    "-   [Arch](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/data-science/arch-autoregressive-conditional-heteroskedasticity/)\\\n",
    "    \\\n",
    "\n",
    "[Difference Between Module, Package, and Library]{style=\"color:red;\"}\n",
    "\n",
    "-   **Module** → A single Python file (`.py` )\n",
    "-   **Package** → A folder containing multiple modules\n",
    "-   **Library** → A collection of packages/modules providing functionality\n",
    "\n",
    "[More](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/what-is-the-difference-between-pythons-module-package-and-library/)\\\n",
    "\\\n",
    "\n",
    "## Modules\n",
    "\n",
    "### Creating a Module\n",
    "\n",
    "To create a module, we write our code and save that in a file with`.py` extension.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example:** Let's create a `calc.py` in which we define two functions, one add and another subtract."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "05b8bd74",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# calc.py\n",
    "def add(x, y):\n",
    "    return (x+y)\n",
    "\n",
    "def subtract(x, y):\n",
    "    return (x-y)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f2fa6bd7",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This is all that is required to create a module.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Importing a Module\n",
    "\n",
    "Modules can be used in another file using the `import` statement. When Python sees an import, it loads the module if it exists in the interpreter’s search path.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Example:** Here, we are importing the **calc** that we created earlier to perform add operation."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "b6ba5a17",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "12\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import calc\n",
    "\n",
    "print(calc.add(10,2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3c1b1084",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Built-in Modules\n",
    "\n",
    "Some common built-in Python modules include `math`, `datetime`, `os`, `sys`, `random`, `statistics`, `collections`, `json`, and `re`. These built-in modules can also be imported using the `import` statement, just as we did with the user-defined module above. Let us consider how functions are imported from a module.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Import Functions from a Module\n",
    "\n",
    "**01. Import One or More Specific Functions from a Module**\n",
    "\n",
    "This allows importing specific functions, classes, or variables rather than the whole module."
